LEC:5- (introducing basic data types)

  int - integers only
  float - fractional numbers with decimals
  
  what differentiates them - integers can be stored in computer memory  as 0’s and 1’s - but fractional numbers are first separated in ‘mantissa’ and ‘exponent’
  
  type(e) - this returns the type(int/float) of expression ‘e’
  
  Boolean (bool) - represents ’true’ and ‘false’ decisions.
  it is determined through comparisons or through logical operators like ‘not, and , or’ - type of operation
  eg- 
  - not true is false, not false is true.
  - x and y are true - if both x, y is true
  - x or y is true - if at least one of x , y is true
  
  using boolean value -
  
  def divides(m,n):
  	if n%m ==0
  		return(True)
  	else:
  		return(False)
  
  def even(n):
  	return(divides(2,n))
  
  def odd(n):
  	return(not divides(2,n))



LEC:6- (strings and operations on strings)

  Manipulation of text - 
    Computation is a lot more than number crunching.
    - text processing is increasingly important ad document preparation, importing/exporting spreadsheet data, matching search queries etc.
  
  Data Type- String (str)
  
    It is a sequence of Characters
    - A single character is a string of length 1
    - Encloses in quotes - single, double, triple
    eg-  city = ‘Chennai’
    title- “hitchhiker’s guide’
  
  
  String as Sequence of characters- 
  
    It is positioned as 0,1,2,….,n-1
    
    eg- s = “hello”
    position- h=0=-1, e=1=-2, l=3=-3, l=4=-4, o=5-5
    
    positions -1, -2 … count backwards from end.
    
  
  Operation on Strings - 
  
    Combining two strings - called concatenation - operator “+”
    
    eg- 
    s = "hello”
    t = s+ “, there”
    print(t)
    -  “hello, there”
    
    
    - we can get length of string using function - len(s)- this will return length of s
    
  
  A slice is a “segment” of a string.
  
    - s = hello
    - s[1:4] is “ell”
    
    Range function in Python is always counted as 
    (1, m+1) is we want the list up till  ‘m’
    
    
    s[:j] starts as s[0], so s[0:,j]
    
    s[i:] ends at s[len(s)-1] so s[i:len(s)]
  
  Modifying strings - 
  
    Cannot update a string ‘in place’ 
    - s = “hello”, we want to change to “help!”
    - if stated - s[3] ‎ =  p - error!
    - Instead we use slices and concatenation
    - s =s[0:3] + “p!”
  - Strings a immutable values - Cannot be changed, without creating a fresh value.




LEC:7 - (lists and operations on lists) - {pic attach +2}

 Lists - 
  
    It is a sequence of characters
    factors = [1,2,5,10]
    names = [“Anand”, “Charles”, “Mohit”] 
    
    Type need not to be uniform - 
    mixed = [3, True, “Yellow”] 
    
    we can extract values as - 
    factors[3] is 10, mixed[0:2] is [3, True]
  
  Difference between Lists and Strings -
  
    For str, both a single position and a slice return strings -
    h = "hello” 
    h[0] ‎ = = h[0:1] == “h”
    
    
    For lists, a single position returns a value, a slice returns a list.
    
    Fac  =  [1,2,5,10]
    Fac[0] is  1, factors[0:1] is  [1]
  
  
  Nested Lists -
  
    nested = [[2,[37]], 4, [“hello”]]
    
    nested[0]  is [2,[37]]
    
    nested[1] is 4
    
    nested[2][0][3] is “l”
    nested[0][1:2] is [[37]]
  
  Updating Lists - 
  
    unlike strings, lists can be updated in place 
    
    nested = [[2,[37]], 4, [“hello”]]
    nested [1] = 7
    nested is now [2, [37]], 7, [“hello”]]
    
    nested[0][1][0]  equal to 19
    nested is now [[2,[19]], 7, “hello”]]
  
  - lists are mutable
  
  
  mutable vs immutable - 
  
    x=5
    y=x
    
    now we change x=7 
    
    has the value of y changed? - nope
    
    no changes were made on y being 5
    
    for immutable values, we can assume that assignment makes a fresh copy of a value
    - values of type int, float, bool, str are immutable
    
    for mutable values, assignment does not make a fresh copy
  
  eg- 
    
    list1= [1,3,5,7]
    list2 = list1
    
    now we change list1[2] = 4
    
    what is list[2][2] now? - it is also 4
    
    here list1 and list2 are two names of the same list.
  
  Copying Lists - 
  
    A slice creates a new (sub) list from an old one.
    full slice - l[0:len(l)]
    
    to make a copy of a list we use a full slice - 
    
    list2 = list1[:]
    
    here any updates made on list 1 will not affect list 2
    
    eg -
    list1 = [1,3,5,7]
    list2 = [list1[:]
    list1[2]= 4
    - list1 = [1,3,4,7]
    - list2 = [1,3,5,7]

  Concatenation - adding lists -
  
    List1 = [1,3,5,7]
    List2 = [4,5,6,8]
    List3 =  List1 + List 2
    
    List 3 is now [1,3,5,7,4,5,6,8]
    
    “+” always produces a new list

